course: dictionary
lesson: 10429
title: Who eats whom
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A new science word often hides its meaning in the words around it. In «A trophic cascade is a change at the top of a food chain that ripples down», the words food chain and who eats whom tell you what trophic is about.
explain: Trophic means to do with food and feeding. A trophic level is the step a living thing has in a food chain: a plant, a plant-eater, or a meat-eater. Ask yourself: where does this animal sit in the chain?
explain: Check your meaning by putting it into the sentence. «Wolves eat elk, elk eat trees, and trees shade streams.» Each link in that chain is a trophic level, so you can picture the whole ladder of who eats whom.
fact: Trophic means to do with food and feeding, especially who eats whom in nature.
fact: A trophic level is the step or position a living thing has in a food chain.
fact: Producers are plants. Primary consumers are plant-eaters, and secondary and tertiary consumers are meat-eaters.
fact: Energy is lost at each trophic level, so there are fewer animals at the top.
fact: A trophic cascade is a change at the top of a food chain that ripples down, like wolves, elk and trees in Yellowstone.
exercise: In «A trophic cascade is a change at the top of a food chain», what does trophic mean? | *To do with food and feeding | To do with weather | To do with the stars | To do with money
exercise: In a food chain of grass, rabbit and fox, what trophic level is the rabbit? | Meat-eater at the top | Plant | Sunlight | *Plant-eater
exercise: Why are there fewer animals at the top of a food chain? | The top animals are the biggest | Plants stop growing at the top | Meat-eaters move house more often | *Energy is lost at each trophic level
test: «Sea otters, sea urchins and kelp are another trophic cascade.» Which one is the plant? | Cascade | Sea urchins | Sea otters | *Kelp
test: Wolves returned to Yellowstone in 1995, and trees grew back. What is a trophic cascade? | A cascade of water over rocks | *A change at the top of a food chain that ripples down | A chain of shops | A change in the weather
exam: In the Yellowstone chain «wolves eat elk, elk eat trees», which living thing is the top meat-eater? | Beavers | *Wolves | Elk | Trees
